#! mathcas 2 notebook

#% title
# cylinder

#% text
# `cylinder([[x1, y1, z1], [x2, y2, z2]], r)` is a solid cylinder of radius r between two points in `graphics3d`.

#% text
# `cylinder()` runs from [0, 0, -1] to [0, 0, 1] with radius 1.

#% section
# Details

#% item
# Coordinates are in the units of the graphic; the box around the scene follows the data unless `plot_range` says otherwise.

#% item
# Colours, `opacity` and `edge_form` before it in its list style it; faces are lit.

#% section
# Examples

#% subsection
# Basic examples

#% text
# The default:

#% cell
graphics3d(cylinder())
#% output
#= image("cylinder.files/cylinder-1.png")

#% subsection
# Scope

#% text
# A thin tilted cylinder:

#% cell
graphics3d(cylinder([[0, 0, 0], [2, 1, 2]], 0.2))
#% output
#= image("cylinder.files/cylinder-2.png")

#% subsection
# Applications

#% text
# Cylinders as the legs of a table:

#% cell
graphics3d([brown, cuboid([0, 0, 1], [2, 1, 1.1]), gray, table(cylinder([[x, y, 0], [x, y, 1]], 0.05), x, [0.1, 1.9], y, [0.1, 0.9])])
#% output
#= image("cylinder.files/cylinder-3.png")

#% section
# See also

#% text
# `graphics3d`, `sphere`, `plot3d`
